Yes. That’s a much better target than “fixing” them.

The aim would be to help someone see, feel, and move through their own golden thread more completely—their continuity across memory, identity, values, relationships, attention, embodiment, and meaning—without forcing them into somebody else’s template.

So the intervention standard becomes:

\text{Does this help the person access more of their own coherent life?}

Not:

* “Does this make them more normal?”
* “Does this make them easier for institutions to manage?”
* “Does this make them resemble the average user?”

But:

\text{person} + \text{aid/environment} \rightarrow \text{greater access to their own thread}

And uniquely matters. Two people can have the same apparent limitation and need completely different “glasses.” One needs filtering. Another needs amplification. Another needs structure. Another needs permission to stop suppressing a trait that was never actually harmful.

That turns assistive design into guided access rather than correction.

The golden thread is not something we manufacture for them. We help them perceive, preserve, and traverse the one that is already theirs.